hat frequency was  doing what at what time  and that's the data that we threw into  the t-sne algorithm  so on this slide at the top left i'm  showing the latent space that we got as  the output of t's knee  for the rest scans and the task scans  shown separately in a and b  and  what we thought was really interesting  is that there's not a lot of overlap yes  i mean you can see overlap with the task  and the rest which is not too surprising  really because  rest is kind of an unconstrained task  but i like that rest is not merely  a sum of all of the different tasks that  you do you see a lot of states present  in rest that are not there in the task  scans you know which suggests that  there's something that's maybe a little  bit different about doing resting state  analysis  than any of these particular tasks at  least  so along with you know how  different um patterns of activity are  distributed in this latent space  where i should remind you that bright  colors here mean that many many time  points  have been placed in the same area  meaning they're very similar  and so those show up as bright spots  um  looking at the same bright spots and the  bottom we can also calculate  how long um a person tends to spend in  any one of those spots  and so high velocity here is shown as a  bright yellow color low glossy is red  and what you notice is people tend to  stick in those high amplitude  areas that we're really calling states  and they move quickly through areas that  don't really have defined states both  for task and rest so that all makes  sense  now if we look at these latent space  distributions and consider them as kind  of like topographical maps right so if  you imagine that these  high density areas that show up in  bright yellow and in the top row  correspond to high mountain peaks  and  things that are blue correspond to low  valleys you can use this watershed  algorithm to segment different areas  from that distribution and that's what  we've done in the middle column and then  you can pick a particular section of  that that we're calling the state  and outline it in pink  and  shown next to it at the far right  is the  um  wavelet coefficient  as a you know as a function of frequency  there so the wavelet decomposition for  that particular state  and you see that for this state at the  top we have a little bit of  lower than average power and some of the  low frequencies whereas for the state at  the bottom we have high power in the  very low frequencies and low power in  the higher frequencies so we can see  distinct patterns across these states  and i'm just going to kind of go through  this quickly i don't want to take too  much time we can separate this out by  task and you see different patterns for  different tasks we also find that the  decomposition is very reproducible  across  scans when we had multiple runs for  example first addressing state and  second resting state  now one of the things we found very  interesting is that if you look at you  know time point by time point within a  particular task you can see differences  um that kind of suggests there might be  these trajectories of activation within  the brain  so here we're looking at two different  components of the um  of one of the tasks with random dots one  where the dots move randomly and one  where they kind of move in a coordinated  manner which we've  labeled as mentalizing on the top  and we can see slight deviations as the  task progresses in the probability  distribution  for those two  types and if we look at the brain states  shown up at the top right we can see  that the main difference between the two  is in the very low frequencies in the  visual cortex but this pattern looking  at now the bottom left this pattern of  kind of progression across the latent  space  gives us a hint that there might be  trajectories in the brain  so when we started thinking about  trajectories the first thing that came  to mind was that maybe we could use a  variational autoencoder  now these are network architectures that  are widely used to take a signal  you force it through a bottleneck where  it's represented by a handful of latent  variables and then you reconstruct those  latent variables through  a symmetric network to try to  recapture the original signal and the  idea is that this gets rid of noise in a  lot of situations so you're trying to  de-noise a signal  using a variational auto encoder  what we were interested in was whether  those latent variables in the bottleneck  can actually capture  fundamental spatiotemporal trajectories  that serve as the building block for the  resting state fmri signal  so  i'm showing here an early approach where  we only use eight orthogonal latent  variables in our reconstruction so you  can ignore the probability distribution  on the left but if you look at the right  now what we've done is encode resting  state fmri from a single individual  during a single scan  where  red is at one end purple is at the other  end and so you're seeing the temporal  progression  of that scan through this latent space  and there again are hints of a  trajectory and i think you can kind of  see you know there's this tendency to go  along the diagonals from  lower left to upper right from upper  left to lower right and maybe that's  something we can pull out  so we ended up using the auto encoder  architecture that i showed as the  example and that we  found by some empirical tests that 32  latent variables worked fairly well  and so when we took the human connectome  project data and fed it through this  auto encoder and then looked at our  latent variables we got some things that  i think are very interesting  so  it's difficult to describe these latent  variables so  inherently they inherently have  dimensions of space by time  and each point has some weighting of  these variables  so the way we've chosen to show them is  for this particular display  we are showing the facial  components  each network  each parcel from each network is a row  and we're showing its activity over time  blue was lower than average activity  yellow is higher than average activity  and basically we've  gotten these plots by setting each  latent variable to its maximum and zero  zeroing out all of the others and then  looking at the reconstructed signal  and so  if you look at the top row you see a lot  of variability over time  in the second row there's less and as  you go down these explain less and less  of the overall variability in the signal  what we also found is we've looked at  the distribution of the spatial  variability for each one of these latent  variables and there should be 32 here  and so if you look  across space which means looking at a  column you'll see a black line for each  of these and that shows where we see the  most variability across the networks at  one time point  and so what we did  is cluster these variables by how  similar their spatial profiles at that  time of maximum variability were  there are a lot of ways to do this this  was just for kind of display purposes  and characterization  i should also say that by comparison  to other data analysis techniques we  found that the first row looks very much  like our primary quasi-periodic pattern  which also has ties to the primary  functional connectivity gradient second  cluster looks like the second functional  connectivity gradient so there are kind  of external validations for these  so here's the same thing now we are  showing for each cluster the spatial  distribution as we go from  a negative value of that particular rate  in variable to a positive value and so  you can see the spatial similarity  really clearly because we're only  showing this now at the time of maximum  spatial variability we indicated in the  previous slide  we can calculate the functional  connectivity  within each of these spatiotemporal  trajectories and we've done that along  the bottom so you can get a feel for  how how different those patterns are  and then in this last one we're showing  the timing of activity and a single roi  that we've picked  and so now you can see along the  vertical axis we have  the value of the latent variable and  along the horizontal axis we have the  timing so you can see for example even  though this first row has common spatial  profiles we actually have very different  temporal behavior for these different  latent variables  so we're very excited about these and  you know  this work is so ongoing i think we have  a lot of things we can do  to explore how well these characterize  resting state fmri data  but i want to have a little time to talk  about another approach that we're using  which is trying to understand the brain  as a complex system and people have been  doing this for a long time and a common  tool that they use is a brain network  model and the way you get one of these  is you take  a graph that describes the network  structure of the brain which we can  obtain for example from  diffusion-weighted fmri  and at each of the nodes each of the  parcels that we make in the brain we  drive it with a set of differential  equations that describes a particular  neural mass model and there are many of  these  and from that we can simulate resting  state fmri data and see how well we  recapitulate features that we see in the  systems level empirical data  now i mentioned that there are many  different models you can use and in fact  one of the problems i i think with this  approach is that many models and many  different parameterizations  give the same  general average functional connectivity  that we see empirically so we needed a  way to  try to  sensitize our analysis a little bit more  and we did that by adding time varying  metrics  again i don't really have time to go  into this too much but i mentioned the  qpps before  so if you look at the top left graph  that's what our qpp looks like from  empirical data and you can see there's  kind of this positive correlation the  negative correlation and area switched  between the two  compared to the brain network models and  we've looked at two different ones here  the firing rate at the  top right and the  choromoto model at the bottom left you  can see both of them catch this  two-state solution but you don't get  this complex propagation between areas  that shows up as a sort of um smeary  pattern in the resting state fmri data  now we did find that the pattern that  you get from these brain network models  depends on  your coupling parameters and so that's  evidence that this dynamic metric is  sensitive to the types of  parameterizations we use and might help  us do a better job  so we we thought we needed a new  approach we have dynamic metrics that do  a better job but we still have those  huge space of parameters that need to be  swept for each model and so it seems  like a great application for machine  learning  and we've we've taken two different  approaches for this and i am going to  walk you through those pretty quickly  here  so the first one is instead of using a  parameter sleep suite let's learn the  initial conditions  and so for this we use the network  architecture based on a long short-term  memory network in lstm which is a  recurrent neural net  and we put in a certain amount of the  data  and  fit that to one of these brain network  models  and tried to use our lstm to estimate  the initial conditions  at the end of that block of data from  the end of that block of data then we  can integrate those differential  equations and see if our path follows  our data  so here it's demonstrated on a spiral  data set where we know exactly which  differential equations were used to  generate the data  and so you can see we do a pretty good  job of fitting it based on those initial  points that we've learned  here we wanted to show that this is also  sensitive to the structural network that  you use  and so at the top left we show the fit  we get when we use the actual  the correct structural network for this  particular  system  and then at top right and bottom left  we're showing the fits that you get if  you use the wrong network  and as you would expect we um  you know we have the same initial  conditions so things start out good but  they diverge from the real activity over  time if we use the wrong structural  network so they are sensitive to that  okay so how does it look when we apply  it to resting state data that's  empirical  well we tried looking at accuracy  as a function of the global coupling  strength that we built in to the model  and what happened was  that the accuracy actually went down as  the global coupling strength went up and  that's shown on the graph on the left  so you know global coupling strength is  usually set to about 0.9 um  when people do parameter sweeps and  that's our worst performer so we  realized what was happening is that  putting any  structural constraints on the data  actually makes it perform worse this is  supported by the fact that if we use  just the lstm  which is um the second point from the  right and that graph  we actually do better than we do with  any structural network at all  so we had another idea  and we thought okay well let's see how  it responds if we mess up the structural  network  and so  um  on the right we're showing the accuracy  when we permuted  um  we've kept only 10 percent of the  original edges of the structural network  and permuted the rest  all the way up to keeping a hundred  percent of the structural network  correct and you can see if we have a low  global coupling parameter like at the  top  we don't really distinguish across these  cases it doesn't matter what we do to  the structural network but if we go to  the high coupling parameter as you see  at the bottom right we start to see this  different as we go  towards that correct structural network  that we think might help us  be more sensitive to which is the right  parameterization to use  so we're very excited about how this is  coming and we think this is going to  help  maybe avoid some of those parameter  sweeps  okay another thing that we can do  so that last approach we learned the  initial conditions and then we  integrated from there that the lstm was  no longer involved  so if you're off on your initial  conditions you're going to  slowly diverge from the signal that  you're trying to model  so what if we update those initial  conditions every time  and so that's what we've done with this  approach  um here we we do the same thing where we  take an initial chunk of the data we put  it through the lstm  we  predict the initial conditions  from there we feed those initial  conditions  we integrate those initiative conditions  to the next point feed it back into the  model  and then do it all again and so what we  get out of this model  is a time course of initial conditions  for each time point of the scan  and so this is actually pretty cool  we've done this with two different  models the firing rate model and the  wilson cohen model and you can see if  you look at the graphs on the left  that the estimated and measured resting  state mri are quite similar  you can also see this in the middle  we're showing  the time course for each of the  different parcels and how well it's  predicted  based on  this lstm model compared to the original  measured measured signal  but what i think is really excited about  this is now we get estimations of  underlying features like excitatory and  inhibitory activity for example with the  wilson cohen model  that we can pull out separately from  this resting state mri data  so i think that will be very cool  so i mentioned before you know that  dynamic  analysis methods really give us the best  distinction between different models and  so we looked at the qpps again this time  from a brain network model that's shown  at bottom left and should look similar  to before you see the two-state solution  the empirical data one is shown at the  top left  and then we've looked at qpps and the  output from these lstms using either the  firing rate model for the middle or the  wilson coen model  on the right at the top  and what you see is that we're starting  to catch some of that more complex  propagation from area to area that we  couldn't get out of that original brain  network model so we think that this is  going to help us fit our data a little  more precisely  so both of those projects are still  clearly ongoing but i think um i'll stop  there with a quick summary i think  machine learning holds huge potential  for helping us to get the most out of  these enormous resting state mri data  sets  and maybe this is a way to get rid of  some of those parameter sweeps maybe  it's a way to make them more accurate  but most of all i think that machine  learning lets us have a data driven  approach that might help us discover new  things  like these latent spatiotemporal  trajectories that we might be able to  use to explain  what's going on in the brain and that  gives us a new way to think about how  activity might be organized across the  whole brain  of course there are always bad things to  go along with the good and i think it's  important not to do just a blast bot's  application of machine learning  where you may not get something that you  can interpret  and  you know i'm sure um  other people will talk about the need to  be careful with the data you know if you  put junk in you're going to get junked  out and you know sometimes i feel like  the enthusiasm for machine learning  gives us a lot of papers that are  published but are very hard to interpret  in any meaningful way  now i i said you know we shouldn't use  machine learning as a black box i think  we've got to tie it to something that we  can interpret and then that makes it a  very powerful tool for us  so some of the things that might be  possible or multimodal measurements  maybe behavioral performance  and of course you know as in anything in  neuroscience reproducibility as a major  issue and i do think it's important to  compare results to existing analysis  methods you know one of our early  iterations of the variational  autoencoder encoder  was great at giving us principal  components which we could get out of a  standard pca analysis  so  you know i think there's no need to use  a fancy machine learning tool if you  have something that's simpler and more  interpretable that will work  now the last thing i want to show is  that you know i said we need to tie  these things to multimodal experiments  and so we're trying to  walk the walk  and  what we are doing is setting up  simultaneous measurements of optical  excitation  inhibition and then fmri  and the goal is to take that wilson  cohen model that we've used and that  we've shown we can learn excitatory and  inhibitory currents from  and test  the activity that we actually measure  optically from genetically targeted  indicators against what we predict from  the resting state fmri acquired at the  same time now we're still working on  this it's technically challenging but i  think it's the right kind of goal to  strive for  and i think the the mere fact of forcing  your machine learning approach to  learning something interpretable  uh opens the opportunity for doing this  type of validation  so i just want to end with an  acknowledgement to my lab especially  amrit kashyap xiaodi zhang who did a lot  of the work that was presented in this  talk  including along with them a former  student jacob billings who is a postdoc  now in czech and did a lot of work on  the t-sne representation  thank you very much for listening and i  look forward to our discussion
 

